e}p$ holds at $W_0$ iff $p$ contains $\delta_S$ for some finite $S$, a set of maps of the constants, all with target $W_0$; Actuality fails at $W_0$, hence at every map, so $
eg$Actuality contains every $\delta_S$ and $\Diamond_{
e}$Actuality fails.

$\Box_{
e}p$ at $W_0$ iff $p$ contains every injective map, and $\hat{\hat\Box}p$ and $\Box_5p$ iff $p$ contains every bijection. With $a$ as in the argument for Actuality, the strongest truth, $\Box_{
e}p\leftrightarrow\Box(\Diamond a	o p)$; $\Diamond a$ fails at every $\langle V,Iangle$, no arrow leading back, and holds at a map $k$ iff $k$ has a left inverse, that is, is injective (every type has constants). As $a$ is the actual world, Proposition D18 of The Bounds of Possibility and the witness $q:=a$ give $\hat{\hat\Box}p\leftrightarrow\Box(\Diamond_{
e}a	o p)$, and $\Diamond_{
e}a$ holds at $k$ iff $k\cdot a=\{m:m\circ k=1_{W_0}\}$ contains an injective $m$, which is then a bijection with inverse $k$: so iff $k$ is a bijection. Actuality gives $\Box_5\le \hat{\hat\Box}$ (actuality-implies-five-implies-two-hat), and the converse is a theorem of C. Now let $j$ be injective and not surjective, say a shift of the individual constants fixing the rest. The proposition $
eg\{j\}$ is in the domain ($\{j\}$ has transports $\{m:m\circ f=j\}$ and $\varnothing$) and contains every bijection but not $j$: it is 5-necessary and two-hat necessary but not ND-necessary. And the truth $a$ is not 5-necessary, missing every bijection but the identity.

At $W_0$ every world sees every world, an arrow $u$ being $(u\circ r)\circ k$ for $r$ a left inverse of $k$; so a true $q$ is possible everywhere, and $\Box_{
e}=\Box$. At $W_1$, writing $I_M$ for the arrows into $W_1$ injective on the fibres over a finite $M\subseteq\mathbb N$: a true proposition contains the arrows into $W_1$ agreeing with the identity on the fibres over some $M$, and the worlds that can reach one of those are exactly $I_M$, so $\Box_{
e}r$ iff $r\supseteq I_M$ for some $M$. Let $w_0$ be the proposition, pinned down by $\varnothing$, that the arrow goes into $W_0$. At a world $h$ into $W_1$, $\Diamond_{
e}w_0$ fails, since there the arrows into $W_1$, which contain $I_M$, make $
eg w_0$ $\Box_{
e}$-necessary; at a world into $W_0$ it holds. So $\Diamond_{
e}w_0=w_0$, while $\Diamond_{
e}q\supseteq w_0$ for every true $q$, and at $W_0$ $\hat{\hat\Box}p$ iff $w_0\subseteq p$. Hence $w_0$ is two-hat necessary but not necessary, so not ND-necessary; and the truth that the first coordinate of the arrow fixes $0$, pinned down by $\{0\}$, is not two-hat necessary.

